It's been a long time since I built a bootable RSX distribution, or even
had to look at one, so the memories are a little hazy, but to the best of
my recollection...

TK50s were definitely bootable by RSX, they were used as distribution
media, as Werner said.

The bootable BRUSYS was not really -11S it was just a special purpose RSX
image.

I believe that the RSX BRU could write a bootable tape, using some
particular commandline switch.  I might be able to find some doc about
that.  The boot ROM reads the first block from the media and examines the
first word or couple of words for a specific bit pattern (instruction
sequence), if that is not found the media is inferred to be non-bootable.

I doubt very much that any VAX utility would write the correct PDP-11
instruction sequence to be recognized as a bootable piece of media.  You
might be able to run the RSX BRU image under VMS (I think this would take
some compatibility support, or VAX-11/RSX?) to get the correct bit pattern
onto the tape.

Just last week I was cleaning out the basement, and uncovered a huge pile
of pumpkin-orange binders, so I suspect I can find some of my old DEC
material - I'll try to take a look for anything useful this weekend to see
what I find.

--Bruce McCulley  
(once upon a time Project Leader for RSX-11M-PLUS v4.0 and other versions)

