Bruce McCulley wrote:
> I believe that the RSX BRU could write a bootable tape, using some
> particular commandline switch.  I might be able to find some doc about
> that.

No you won't, because BRU can't do that.  VMR, on the other
hand, can do it - for example:

                  >SET /UIC=[6,54]
                  >RUN VMRM41
                  ENTER FILENAME:BRUSYS
                  VMR>SAVE MU:BRUSYS
                  VMR>(ctrl/Z)

VMR is also used to build RX01's and RX02's with standalone BRU, as
well as tapes and floppies with standalone DSC.

> The boot ROM reads the first block from the media and examines the
> first word or couple of words for a specific bit pattern (instruction
> sequence), if that is not found the media is inferred to be non-bootable.

Only on the very, very earliest bootable tapes is the first block
read from the tape and executed.  From the late 70's onward, most
bootable tapes from DEC are ANSI tapes, and thus have an actual
ANSI label on the tape before the boot block.  You find many
non-DEC bootable tapes - such as Unix distributions - with two
copies of the boot block at the beginning so that either method
works.  (Alas, that's about the sum total of support as you find for
ANSI standards in Unix...)

On Q-bus and later Unibus machines, the magic pattern that has
to be at the front is a NOP followed by a BR around the vectors
at 4 and 10.  Earlier Unibus boot ROMs didn't really care - they
just read in the first or second record and executed it.

> I doubt very much that any VAX utility would write the correct PDP-11
> instruction sequence to be recognized as a bootable piece of media.  You
> might be able to run the RSX BRU image under VMS (I think this would take
> some compatibility support, or VAX-11/RSX?) to get the correct bit pattern
> onto the tape.

VAX-11/RSX, with a copy of VMR and BRUSYS.* from [6,54], is
indeed capable of making a standalone BRU tape.  After all, there's
the capability of doing an entire SYSGEN under VAX-11/RSX.

