Hello RSX fans,
with your answers my brain becomes refreshed.

You have to distinguish between RSX disk distribution medium disk as RL02 and tape
or diskette as TK50 or RX50.
The disk distribution contained a bootable disk with a base system which was able to
run the sysgen.
A tape or diskette distribution started with a bootable BRUSYS which restored a base
system to disk which was bootable and able to run the sysgen.

I remember TK50 distribution tapes which started with a BRUSYS image followed by a
BRU image for a system to restore to a DU: device (Micro RSX11Mplus for MicroPDP11).

BRUSYS is a RSX11S System with all necessary utilities (INI, BAD, BRU) to prepare a
blank disk and to restore an system image back to it. Because RSX11S has no
filesystem BRU is only capable to do an image restore, no file restores.

The "Install" diskette (or tape) of a MicroRSX is a RSX11S System with an easy user
interface (guide) to do the preparation and restoration of the system disk. In
principle it does the same task as a BRUSYS system, but in an easy and guided way.

How to get a bootable BRUSYS medium?
On a running RSX11M or RSX11Mplus system you'll find in the directory [6,54] a file
"BRUSYS.SYS". You have to use VMR (MCR = Monitor Console Routine => the command
language of a running RSX11 system, VMR = Virtual MCR => the command language of a
RSX11 image file) to complete (install all necessary device drivers and tasks e.g.
to a system image) the image and to save (SAV ddx:)  it to a medium such as diskette
or tape.
I suggest to create the BRUSYS system on diskette (even to restore a tape TK50
system to disk) because it boots much faster as a TK50 BRUSYS.
VMR writes the bootable system to a raw device. (I don't think that any VMS- or
restore utility is able to read this medium, it has no format). It starts with 2
blocks � 512 byte overhead and secondary boot code, the following blocks are the
memory image which is started after reading into memory. To make this procedure
hardware independant (q-bus map or unibus map) the read procedure of the image was
limited by DIGITAL to 124k Words.
(Btw December '87 I published at DECUS a patch to two versions ov VMR (RSX11M V4.2D
patch2 and RSX11M+ V3.0E pregenned kit patch1) which allowed an image size of the
number of blocks -2 of the medium for Q22 machines.)

Greetings to all RSX people
Werner

PS: Excuse my limited knowledge of english language. If you have any further
questions I try to remember and to answer.

